INT. DRAINED PUBLIC SWIMMING POOL - NIGHT

Deep end. Cracked blue tile. Moonlight leaks through a broken skylight.

DEZ OKAFOR, 40s, shaved head, sits on an upturned lifeguard chair. Duffel bag between his boots.

ROZ VANCE, 30s, leather jacket, thumb on a revolver hammer. She stands over the drain.

ROZ
You're late.

DEZ
Traffic.

ROZ
At two in the morning.

DEZ
There was a parade.

ROZ
For who?

DEZ
Me. Confetti and everything. Count it.

ROZ
You count it. I want to see your hands doing the counting.

DEZ
My hands are fine. My hands have never been the problem.

ROZ
Your mouth is the problem.

DEZ
My mouth got us this pool. Free rent, no neighbours, a diving board for a lookout.

ROZ
It smells like a wet dog died in a bleach factory.

DEZ
That's the ambience. You pay extra for that.

ROZ
I'm not paying extra for anything. I'm paying what we agreed.

DEZ
Then count.

Steel door CLANGS above. Boots on the ladder.

TOBIAS KRELL, 50s, soaked raincoat, grey stubble, drops to the tile. Revolver out. Shaking.

TOBIAS
Nobody move.

ROZ
Tobias, put that down.

DEZ
Who's Tobias?

ROZ
My brother-in-law.

TOBIAS
Ex.

ROZ
Ex-brother-in-law. He drove.

DEZ
You brought a driver to a deal?

TOBIAS
I brought a gun to a deal!

ROZ
You brought a gun that doesn't have bullets, Tobias.

TOBIAS
...How did you know that?

ROZ
I took them out. Last Tuesday.

Tobias lowers the gun. Laughs, thin and relieved. Sits on the tile.

TOBIAS
Thank God. I thought I'd have to use it.

DEZ
Count the money, Roz.
